From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 14:47:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(control): WT knows when the application asked for
 bracketed paste (GD-08)

Patch 50. BracketedPasteEnabled returned a hardcoded false.

The issue text said this meant WT never wraps a paste in ESC[200~ /
ESC[201~. That part was wrong, and worth recording. ghostty_surface_text
is documented as "treated like a paste" and it is - it lands in
completeClipboardPaste, which frames the data whenever mode 2004 is on.
Pastes have been correctly fenceposted since the first ghostty pane. A
wrap added here would have been the second one.

What false actually cost is everything WT decides *around* the paste,
all of it above the core and all of it reading this one property:
TrimPaste trimmed text it should have left alone, an empty clipboard
sent nothing where it should send bare fenceposts, and the multi-line
warning fired on every multi-line paste - including into applications
that had said, in as many words, that they can tell a paste from typing.

The empty-clipboard case needs the extra branch in PasteText. It only
became reachable now: TerminalPage discards an empty paste outright when
it believes the mode is off, so the load-bearing empty paste never got
that far before. libghostty returns early on zero-length input before
its encoder runs, so the bare pair is written straight to the
connection.

Measured in a real ghostty pane with a raw-input probe, mode 2004 on:

  HELLO          -> <ESC>[200~HELLO<ESC>[201~
  AAA\r\nBBB     -> <ESC>[200~AAA<CR><LF>BBB<ESC>[201~, no warning dialog
  (empty)        -> <ESC>[200~<ESC>[201~

with warning.multiLinePaste set to automatic, so the multi-line paste
arriving unattended is itself the evidence the flag is read.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 39 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 88e1ccc85..6b6f1f3a5 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -838,6 +838,30 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
     void GhosttyControlCore::PasteText(const winrt::hstring& hstr)
     {
+        // Non-empty text needs nothing from us: ghostty_surface_text is
+        // libghostty's paste entry point, so it already fenceposts the data
+        // when mode 2004 is on and strips the bytes xterm strips. Adding our
+        // own ESC[200~ here would send two of them.
+        //
+        // Empty text is the exception, and it is not a no-op. An empty
+        // bracketed paste is how an application learns a paste happened with
+        // nothing on the clipboard - WT's own core calls that load-bearing and
+        // GNOME Terminal does the same. libghostty returns early on
+        // zero-length input before the encoder ever sees it, so the bare pair
+        // has to come from here.
+        //
+        // Reachable only since BracketedPasteEnabled started telling the
+        // truth: TerminalPage discards an empty paste outright when it thinks
+        // the mode is off, which it always did before.
+        if (hstr.empty())
+        {
+            if (BracketedPasteEnabled())
+            {
+                WriteToConnection("\x1b[200~\x1b[201~");
+            }
+            return;
+        }
+
         SendInput(hstr);
     }
 
@@ -1598,7 +1622,20 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     bool GhosttyControlCore::ForceCursorVisible() const noexcept { return false; }
     void GhosttyControlCore::ForceCursorVisible(bool /*force*/) {}
 
-    bool GhosttyControlCore::BracketedPasteEnabled() const noexcept { return false; }
+    // Whether the application has turned on DEC mode 2004.
+    //
+    // This is not what frames a paste. ghostty_surface_text is documented as
+    // "treated like a paste", so libghostty has been wrapping our pastes in
+    // ESC[200~/ESC[201~ correctly since the first ghostty pane - PasteText
+    // must not add a second pair. What was missing is WT *knowing*, and three
+    // things above the core read it: whether to trim the pasted text
+    // (TrimPaste), whether an empty clipboard still sends bare fenceposts, and
+    // whether to raise the multi-line paste warning. All three were deciding
+    // as though every application were paste-blind.
+    bool GhosttyControlCore::BracketedPasteEnabled() const noexcept
+    {
+        return _surface && ghostty_surface_bracketed_paste_enabled(_surface);
+    }
     void GhosttyControlCore::ClearBuffer(Control::ClearBufferType /*clearType*/) {}
     // The whole screen, scrollback included. GHOSTTY_POINT_SCREEN counts from
     // the start of the scrollback rather than the viewport, and the TOP_LEFT /
